                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Doyen clamps commonly have long, gently curved or straight jaws, with designs varying by manufacturer. Label the ratchet and finger rings only if present on the displayed instrument."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Material: Commonly surgical-grade stainless steel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Length: Usually long enough to span a segment of bowel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Jaws: Long, relatively slender, and designed for atraumatic occlusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Jaw surfaces: May have longitudinal grooves or other patterns designed to distribute pressure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Joint: Allows the jaws to open and close."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Locking mechanism: Many models have a ratchet to maintain closure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Shape: Available with straight or curved jaws."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterilization: Reusable models require appropriate cleaning and validated sterilization."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The main design objective is to occlude the bowel while reducing direct tissue trauma compared with a crushing clamp."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/doyen_intestinal_clamp_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "1. Straight Doyen intestinal clamp"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has straight jaws and is useful when the operative anatomy permits straightforward placement along the bowel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/doyen_intestinal_clamp_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "2. Curved Doyen intestinal clamp"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has curved jaws that may facilitate positioning around bowel segments in selected operative fields."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Doyen clamps may also vary in jaw length, curvature, and overall size. The instrument's exact design should be identified from its appearance and manufacturer's description."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The Doyen clamp works by applying controlled, distributed pressure to temporarily occlude the bowel lumen."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The appropriate bowel segment is identified."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The clamp is positioned across the intended segment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The jaws are closed gently and secured according to the instrument design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The applied pressure limits passage of intestinal contents through the clamped segment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The clamp is released when occlusion is no longer required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The long jaws distribute the applied pressure over a relatively broad area, reducing localized compression compared with a narrow, strongly crushing clamp."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Occlude the bowel adequately while avoiding unnecessary pressure or prolonged clamping."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The Doyen intestinal clamp may be used to:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Temporarily occlude the bowel lumen."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduce spillage of intestinal contents during selected bowel operations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Isolate a segment of intestine during resection."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assist during bowel transection and anastomosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Temporarily control the passage of intestinal contents during selected reconstructive procedures."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_8",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which it is used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Procedure",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Small bowel resection",
                      "Temporarily occlude bowel segments during resection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Colectomy",
                      "Occlude selected bowel segments during transection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Intestinal anastomosis",
                      "Help control bowel contents while bowel ends are prepared and joined"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Selected emergency laparotomies",
                      "Isolate bowel segments during appropriate procedures"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bowel reconstruction",
                      "Assist temporary occlusion during reconstruction"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise application depends on the operation, bowel condition, and surgeon's technique."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Atraumatic design: Intended to reduce crushing injury compared with crushing intestinal clamps."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Long jaws: Allow occlusion across a relatively long segment of bowel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Distributed pressure: Helps reduce focal pressure on the bowel wall."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Temporary occlusion: Limits passage of intestinal contents during selected procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reusability: Many models can be cleaned and sterilized for repeated use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ease of handling: The clamp mechanism allows controlled application and release."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Excessive pressure can still injure the bowel wall."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Prolonged occlusion may compromise tissue perfusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Incorrect positioning may fail to occlude the lumen adequately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A clamp may slip if poorly positioned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It may not be suitable for all bowel conditions, especially severely friable or oedematous tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its long jaws may be cumbersome in confined operative spaces."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Bowel wall injury",
                      "Excessive compression may damage the intestinal wall"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ischaemia",
                      "Prolonged or excessive pressure may compromise bowel perfusion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Serosal injury",
                      "The outer bowel layer may be damaged during clamping"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Perforation",
                      "Severe tissue injury may lead to bowel perforation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Leakage or spillage",
                      "Inadequate occlusion may allow intestinal contents to escape"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Clamp slippage",
                      "Poor positioning may cause loss of occlusion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue oedema",
                      "Compression may contribute to local tissue swelling"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Anastomotic complications",
                      "Bowel injury or compromised tissue viability may adversely affect healing"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_12",
              "group_title": "Prevention",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use an appropriate atraumatic clamp for the tissue and procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the bowel and select a suitable site for occlusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Close the clamp only as much as required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid excessive pressure and unnecessary clamping duration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check that the clamp remains correctly positioned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the bowel for injury or impaired perfusion after release."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Replace damaged or poorly functioning clamps."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Doyen clamp versus crushing intestinal clamp",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a high-yield OSCE distinction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Doyen intestinal clamp",
                    "Crushing intestinal clamp"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Main purpose",
                      "Temporary, relatively atraumatic occlusion",
                      "Firm compression and occlusion"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pressure",
                      "Designed to distribute pressure and minimize tissue injury",
                      "Intentionally applies greater crushing pressure"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Effect on bowel wall",
                      "Aims to preserve tissue integrity",
                      "May damage the clamped segment"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical role",
                      "Occlusion of bowel segments during intestinal surgery",
                      "Clamping a segment intended for division or removal, depending on the procedure"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue preservation",
                      "Preferred when preservation of the clamped bowel is important",
                      "The clamped tissue may be unsuitable for retention, depending on the clamp and technique"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: Do not assume every clamp with long jaws is atraumatic. Jaw pattern, pressure characteristics, and intended use determine the instrument's classification."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Precautions and contraindications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few absolute contraindications specific to the instrument, but careful selection is essential."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid excessive pressure on fragile or diseased bowel."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use particular caution in oedematous, inflamed, or ischaemic intestine."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid unnecessary prolonged clamping."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm adequate occlusion without over-tightening."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use a crushing clamp only when the operative technique specifically calls for it."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not use a damaged clamp or one with an unreliable locking mechanism."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For reusable Doyen clamps:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly after use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open the jaws and ratchet fully to expose the joint."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Clean the jaw surfaces, box lock, shanks, and any grooves thoroughly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Rinse and dry according to the manufacturer's instructions."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect for damaged jaw alignment, corrosion, and locking failure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm that the jaws open and close smoothly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sterilize using a validated method compatible with the instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Residual biological material around the joint or jaw surfaces can interfere with effective cleaning and reprocessing."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a Doyen intestinal clamp?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An atraumatic intestinal clamp used to temporarily occlude the bowel lumen during gastrointestinal surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is its main principle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Controlled, distributed pressure to occlude the bowel while minimizing crushing injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why are its jaws long?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To distribute pressure over a relatively long bowel segment and facilitate temporary occlusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Is it a crushing or non-crushing clamp?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is generally classified as an atraumatic or non-crushing intestinal occlusion clamp."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Mention three operations in which it may be used."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Small bowel resection, colectomy, and intestinal anastomosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Mention four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bowel wall injury, ischaemia, serosal injury, and inadequate occlusion with intestinal spillage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why should prolonged clamping be avoided?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Prolonged compression can compromise bowel perfusion and cause tissue injury."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the difference between a Doyen clamp and a crushing bowel clamp?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A Doyen clamp is designed for relatively atraumatic temporary occlusion, whereas a crushing clamp applies greater pressure and may damage the clamped bowel segment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What should be inspected before use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Jaw alignment, jaw surfaces, joint movement, ratchet function if present, and any signs of corrosion or damage."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. What should be checked after releasing the clamp?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The bowel wall should be assessed for visible injury and appropriate tissue perfusion."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "OSCE examiner's checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
