                    "States its role in bowel resection and anastomosis",
                    "Distinguishes it from a crushing intestinal clamp",
                    "Mentions advantages and disadvantages",
                    "Identifies bowel injury and ischaemia risks",
                    "Explains how to minimize pressure-related injury",
                    "Describes inspection, cleaning and sterilization",
                    "Explains what to assess after clamp release"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "doyen_intestinal_clamp_group_18",
              "group_title": "Final high-yield summary",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Identification: Long-jawed intestinal occlusion clamp."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main parts: Handles, shanks, joint, jaws, and ratchet if present."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Principle: Distributed pressure for temporary bowel occlusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Main uses: Bowel resection, transection, and anastomosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Major risks: Bowel wall injury, ischaemia, and leakage from inadequate occlusion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key distinction: Doyen is designed to minimize crushing injury; a crushing intestinal clamp applies stronger compression."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}